//! Stable shell execution policy delivered with the tool, across roles and models.

pub(super) const DESCRIPTION: &str = "Run a POSIX shell command. Start defaults to sandbox_access=isolated. Execution environment: isolated uses the configured filesystem sandbox, private HOME, filtered environment variables, and isolated networking. network allows host networking for this command while keeping the same filesystem mounts, private HOME, and filtered environment; it does not restore host configuration, credentials, agent sockets, or environment variables. unsandboxed removes filesystem/network isolation and inherits the evorch process environment (which may differ from a login terminal), with explicit tool environment overrides. Select the narrowest mode that supplies the resources the command actually needs: network for connectivity alone, unsandboxed when host files, configuration, credentials, sockets, or environment are needed. You may request the appropriate mode directly when that need is already known. Both network and unsandboxed require a nonempty justification and review before execution; review may be automatic or require a user response, or escalation may be disabled. A request is not an approval. Failure diagnosis: an error describes this command in its selected execution environment, not the whole host. Distinguish connectivity, filesystem access, HOME/configuration, environment, authentication, and command failures. Before declaring a resource missing or asking the user to install, log in, supply secrets, or run the command manually, check whether isolation hides it and use the reviewed access path when justified by the user's task. Do not repeat a deterministic failure under unchanged conditions or reset unrelated configuration. If review denies the request, use the exact returned reason; do not assume a GUI prompt, user refusal, timeout, or missing capability, and do not bypass the denial. Omitting yield_ms waits for completion, but model tool calls automatically return a continuing run-owned job after 10000 ms or host input; the command is not restarted. This releases the shell wait, not necessarily the next model request if other tools are still running. Such starts close stdin (also PTY EOF); set an explicit yield_ms to retain stdin for interactive use. Explicit start yield_ms (0..1800000, up to 30 minutes) waits for completion or its deadline, ignoring output. Poll defaults to 60000 ms and waits for completion or the deadline, NOT new output; omit yield_ms for this completion wait. For prompts, explicitly select poll return_on:output; stdin preserves output-or-completion waiting. Zero returns immediately; stdin/stop default to zero and accept up to 60000 ms. Poll accepts up to 1800000 ms. Output never extends a deadline. Omit cursor to continue after output already returned (a running poll deadline favors bounded tail output and flags skipped bytes); pass an explicit cursor, including 0, for ordered paging. Repeated identical immediate polls can stop the run, so prefer the default completion wait. To cancel use action:stop with yield_ms:1000, then poll to terminal completion if necessary. Stop does not roll back filesystem effects. Jobs retain their sandbox/cwd/environment, stop when the run ends, and cannot resume after restart. Live output contains complete lines; long output has a bounded temporary artifact on completion. Control actions retain the job's original access and cannot set sandbox_access; stdin for a reviewed job requires a fresh review.";

pub(super) const ACCESS_DESCRIPTION: &str = "Start only. isolated: configured filesystem/network isolation, private HOME, filtered environment. network: reviewed host networking only; filesystem mounts, private HOME, and filtered environment stay isolated, so host configuration/credentials/sockets are not restored. unsandboxed: reviewed host filesystem/network access and inherited evorch process environment. Choose by required resources, not just the command's name. network and unsandboxed require a nonempty justification; denial is returned with its reason.";
